How to set up a development environment?
========================================

0. switch to development environment with:

       cp config/build-local_tmpl.properties config/build-local.properties

1. In the file
       logon-persistence/etc/csv/testdata/config.csv
   add a key 'mail.test.to' for the instance 'adnovum.dev.<login>' (e.g.
   'adnovum.dev.marius') with your own e-mail address (see existing entries).
   This ensures that mails sent by the application from your instance are only
   sent to yourself and not to everyone on the mailing list.
   -> See for example change TEST458.

2. then build the whole project by calling "adnant" on the top level

3. deploy and run the project
   1. call "adnant container-coldstart" on the toplevel
      - installs a container
      - configures a container setup for the project
      - starts server, will not return as long as the app server is running.
        (Easy app server status check, easy restart with ctrl-c)

   2. deploy the project
      - call 'adnant int-deploy' (or another 'xxx-deploy' target for a different zone)
	  (See https://aww.adnovum.ch/confluence/display/HolcimPegasus/HolcimEnvironmentEBM
	  for an overview of the different zones. If you are not sure, 'int-deploy' is
	  probably OK.


How to add the project to Eclipse
=================================

 1. execute this on the top level of the project:
     adnant init

The LOGON app consists of several modules which are subprojects.
It is convenient to add them all as separate projects to Eclipse:

 2. File > Import ... > General > Existing Projects into Workspace
 3. Set root directory: /home/<user>/projects/logon <Tab>
 4. All subprojects should appear. Select all and finish.

In order to add the project itself to Eclipse do the following:

 5. File > New Project... > General > Project > Next
 6. Project name: logon > Finish

Now, configure a new working set:

 7. Project -> Build Working Sets -> Select Working Set...
    click New, select java, next
 8. working set name: logon
    working set contents: select all subprojects that belong to logon
    -> click finish
 9. select logon, click ok

10. open the package explorer menu (in package explorer, upper right
    corner, click on arrow which points down), 'configure working sets'

11. select logon, click ok

Logon is now conveniently available under the logon working set


About the project structure:
============================

The project consists of the following sub-projects:

logon-init-web                   Provides a servlet to reload the configuration
logon-admin-web                  Web UI for the administration part
logon-cust-web                   Web UI for 'webbestellung' and password change
logon-order-web                  Web UI for the order part
logon-terminal-web               UI for the touch screen terminal

logon-webservices                (WS server) all SAP interfaces
logon-webservices-mop-incoming   (WS server) WS service for mobile devices
logon-webservices-pws-incoming   (WS server) WS service for the parallel weighting system
logon-webservices-client         (WS client) logon as a client of other web services (fax, sms, logon->PDA)

logon-common-web                 Functionality common to all web subprojects

logon-business                   Business tier (services as EBJs)
logon-persistence                DB Model, patches, configuration, and refdata

logon-common                     Functionality common to all subprojects

logon-security                   Login modules for all packages (dmz/dev/lan)

logon-application                takes all components, builds the ear

logon-simulator                  For manual testing of web services using SoapUI
logon-test-load                  Tools for load testing the application


You can enable/disable the build of each of these subprojects by way
of adjusting your config/build-local.properties file:

---
# uncomment if you do want to omit a component to build:
omit.subcomponent.webservices=true
omit.subcomponent.webservices-mop-incoming=true
---

please be aware that some subprojects depend on other subprojects.

                            depends on:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
logon-common:               -
logon-common-web:           logon-common
logon-persistence:          logon-common
logon-business:             logon-common, logon-persistence
logon-admin-web:            logon-common, logon-common-web, logon-business
logon-cust-web:             logon-common, logon-common-web, logon-business
logon-init-web:             logon-common, logon-common-web, logon-business
logon-order-web:            logon-common, logon-common-web, logon-business
logon-terminal-web:         logon-common, logon-common-web, logon-business
logon-webservices:          logon-common
logon-webservices-mop-incoming:      logon-common, logon-persistence
logon-webservices-pws-incoming:      logon-common, logon-persistence
logon-webservices-client:   logon-common, logon-persistence
logon-security:				logon-common, logon-business
logon-simulator:			-
logon-test-load:            -
logon-application:          *


Samples and patterns:
=====================

Here are some hint on how to find answers to questuions such as:

- How do I add a new Service, Actions, etc.


Add a new Service:
------------------

- create a new java file like this, replacing 'SalesArea' with the name you want:
	@Stateless
	public class SalesAreaServiceBean extends SalesAreaServiceDispatcher
	(don't care if the referred dispatcher is not existing)
- cd logon-business; aa generate -> this will generate the dispatcher and the service interface
- you can build the whole project, should compile

Add a new EntryAction/Action/Form:
----------------------------------

- IMPORTANT: every screen should have a corresponding EntryAction/Action
   - the entryactions *only* care about things that are displayed on the screen
     (fetches data, makes sure all the refdata, etc. is around to display)
   - the normal actions handle user actions (user clicks on save, delete, etc)

- to add a new EntryAction/Action/Form-Combo
   - copy paste&rename: FunctionOneEntryAction -> YourEntryActio
   - copy paste&rename: FunctionOneAction -> YourAction
   - copy paste&rename: FunctionOneForm -> YourForm
   - make sure that you adjust *all* the xdoclet annotations as well
   - do NOT edit struts-config.xml, web.xml, etc: These are generated with xdoclet

How to add a new view:
----------------------

The views should be named like this:

    VLGN[R|C|U|S]_SOMENAME(without_VIEW at the end)

-> choose R|C|U|S depending on the use case (Refdata, Common, User, SAP)

Steps:

- add the new view to the DB and to logon-persistence/etc/sql/custom_views.sql
- make sure that the *first* attribute of the view is a unique identifier!
  if you don't have a single id with this property, you can do something like this:

  CREATE OR REPLACE FORCE VIEW VLGNR_SHIPTO_TYPE" (...) AS
  SELECT
    bs.SHIPTO_TYPE_CD || '_' || tx.LANG_CD COMPOSITE_ID,
    ...

- then:
   cd logon-persistence
   adnant oraviews
     -> this will update the files in 'logon-persistence/etc/database/views', by generating
     the definition xmls to the views (including your new one) in the database set in
     db.connection.url. These definition xmls will be the base for generating entity classes.

- adnant clean dist in logon-persistence.
  Execute the grant for ulgn02 for your new view, found in
    logon-persistence/build/generated/gdi/schema2ddlentities/sql/create/45_gen_grant_privileges.sql

- build the project and if you're satisfied, check in the new file in 'logon-persistence/etc/database/views'

  Please check in ONLY the modified view-mapping file where you expect changes!
  Reason: adnant oraviews depends on the DB, and the Dev DB may be different
  than a fresh DB built from SVN head. This periodic clean/update is currently
  done together with releases to the customer. Soon, a Nightly Build task will
  display differences


About properties and config
---------------------------

NOTE: LogonProperties are defined in the properties files 'logon.properties'
and 'buildtime.properties'

it contains properties which are either defined at build time or can not
be defined in the database, such as which 'instance' the application is
running as.

All other configuration parameters are retrieved from the DB and are
accessible through ConfigBean (accessible through ConfigService)

NOTE: ConfigBean contains the application configuration values (which are
retrieved from the DB). ServiceSupport offers getConfigBean() to get this
bean. ConfigBean values can be overriden by setting system properties. e.g.
    -Dapp.default.shippingpoint_shipto.GeofenceRadius_m=somevalue

You can look at the current configuration like this:

http://localhost:8080/logon/init/versionCheck

You can use this screen also to change the configuration or to update
the configuration if it was changed on the DB.

How to add a property: Whenever you add (or change, delete) properties:

1. add the property to: logon-persistence/etc/csv/refdata/config.csv
2. adnant dist in logon-persistence
3. execute the generated ./logon-persistence/build/generated/gdi/csv2sql/refdata/insert_config.sql


How to create a new patch file for a new version
=======================================================

Run logon-persistence/etc/sql/patch/create_new_patch.sh.


How to add database columns and configuration variables
=======================================================
(mail from emoke on 1-Oct-2012)

DB CHANGES
-----------------------
(Probably it's described in the README, probably not, we usually suggest
to take a look at ChangeLog entries how people do such things.)

Adding a new field is basically very easy, currently what you need to do is:

1. edit the xml file of the table, add manually the fields, write a
meaningful comment (in your case tlgnu_user.xml)

2. run adnant clean dist in logon-persistence -> this will do for you
several things listed below

2a. this will regenerate
/logon-persistence/build/generated/gdi/adxml2ddl/sql/create/10_gen_create_tables.sql
and all the other sql files present in
/logon-persistence/build/generated/gdi/adxml2ddl/sql/create. If you
added foreign key, then things are generated into other files, like fk.
etc. etc.

2b. this will regenerate the Entity which belongs to the table in your
case UserEntity

3. here comes tool of Urs
-- Patching the entities for the main schema
-- HINT: look at helper tool "logon-persistence/logon_alter_db.sh" !
this helps to find all related rows which need to be added into the
current patch which is patch_above_2.1.0 from those files which were
generated by step 2a.

4. if you added a column to a table having some initial data, modify
the initial data scripts, input files for all clusters (files like
sarea.sql.vm, sarea.csv, tlgnc_sarea.sql etc).

Commits you can use as pattern:
PERSIST1905 (very simple fields added, no foreign keys or things like
that, I hope yours is like that).
r11192 - initial data (sarea)
r11193 - initial data (sap material)


CONFIG VALUES
-----------------------

For adding config value PERSIST1720
Here you need to add the config value to a csv file, after generation
(adnant clean dist) you will find our insert statements in
/logon/logon-persistence/build/generated/gdi/csv2sql/refdata/insert_config.sql
WATCH OUT there is also one in testdata folder, where adnovum configs
are stored. We need those too.

In the patch you don't need to add the new config inserts as they are
loaded, see in patch_above_2.1.0.0

-- Holcim Prod & Quality environment, AdNovum integration environment:
@@../../refdata/insert_config.sql
-- AdNovum Test environment ('dev'):
-- @@../../testdata/insert_config.sql -- DO NOT EXECUTE AS PART OF A
PATCH, always execute manually!


WARNING
-----------------------
1. If you have committed the xml/csv file's changes, don't forget to
update all dev databases we have for Logon:
see https://aww.adnovum.ch/twiki/bin/view/HolcimPegasus/LogonDatabasesOverview
for up-to-date information

2. Currently the login module is in state, when there is a generated
login module for dev environment and there is the normal one. I did not
really read you ticket through, but probably you will need to do things
in the login module, so I'd rather talk to the those people who are
familiar with the login module like Lajos(lajos), and Sanyi (bartha).
Perhaps changing the user table requires other things also not just
these described above. I'm sure Urs will anyway know if you need to do
such things where you need to be careful. Let's hope that the steps
described above are enough. :)




How to maintain the eventbeans?
===============================

1. edit eventbeans.xml manually, if you want to make structural changes in MOP events.
2. execute adnant dist to generate event bean classes (like LoadOrder.java) out of it

How to insert tracing code? (minimal survival guide)
===========================

You can 'instrument' your code with:

    adnant adntrace2-instrument

or more selectively:

    adnant -Dclass=YourClassNameHere adntrace2-instrument
    adnant -Dmethod=YourMethodNameHere adntrace2-instrument

If you only want to 'refresh' already existing tracing code, you can use:

    adnant adntrace2-refresh

This doesn't add any new tracing code, it only refreshes existing
tracing code which is helpful when parameters have changed.

adntrace2 directives in java source code:

    @adnlog.notrace
    No instrumentation for this method. This tag has no attributes.

    @adnlog.exclude-all
    Deselects all parameters and return value from tracing.

    @adnlog.exclude parameter1, return
    Deselects specific parameters and/or return value. This example would exclude the
    parameter with identifier "parameter1" and the return value.

How to use soapUI
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1.  start soapUI (the target is only availabe in the logon-webservices-client and logon-webservices-mop-incoming component)
    adnant start-soapui

2.1 Open tests for logon-webservices-mop-incoming
    logon-webservices-mop-incoming/src/test/resources/mop-incoming-soapui-project.xml
    adnant test-soapui (to run the tests inside the soapui project)

2.2 Open one of the logon-webservices-client test cases (go inside the logon-webservices-client folder)
	adnant filter-soapui-project-template (this creates the soapui project files from the templates)
    logon-webservices-client/build/test/etc/logon-ws-client-soapui-project.xml
    logon-webservices-client/build/test/etc/mop-gateway-soapui-project.xml
    The template files (test/mockup/*_template.xml cannot be edited with the soapui, use the generate files and remerge the your changes (ask zsolt about this ;))
    Both files contain mock services for the services logon is a client (sms, paralell, fax, mop, ...)

3.  Start working on the test, there is a good User Guide on the soapUI homepage
    http://www.soapui.org/userguide/index.html

4.  Make sure you saved all the tests before you close soapui again
    Commit the changes of the soapui project files (there is a place where your username is encoded, but this is not critical)

Running integration-tests:
----------------------------------
adnant test-integration
	from top-level of the project: runs all the integration-tests of all of the sub-projects
	from a specific sub-project: runs all the integration-tests of the sub-project
adnant test-integration -Dtest.class=YourSeleniumTest.java
	runs a certain integration-test YourSeleniumTest (path must be given at this point, will be improved soon)
	e.g. ~/projects/logon/logon-admin-web> adnant test-integration -Dtest.class=ShippingpointTest.java


NOTE:
  - The README must provide a simple jumpstart into the development setup of the project. It targets
    engineers being familiar with the adnovum development environment and focuses on project specifics.

  - To use coverage set the property "coverage.enabled" in your build.properties (see uncommented
    section) or simply call adnant with the command line argument "-Dcoverage.enabled=true"
    e.g.
    adnant -Dcoverage.enabled=true
    adnant test-unit -Dcoverage.enabled=true
    adnant test-integration -Dcoverage.enabled=true (this uses container and DB already to be started)

  - 'adnant container-start' and 'adnant container-coldstart' will NOT return as long as the app server is
    running. To stop the server, simply press 'ctrl-c'.

  - To start the container spawned simply set jvm.spawn=true in your build-local.properties.

How to syntax check (and execute) JPA QL?
=========================================
If you have to write JPA QL queries, you will want to syntax check them without
having to build the application. There's a jpaconsole for this:

1. start the jpaconsole from project top level:
   cd $HOME/projects/logon
   adnant jpaconsole
2. start an editor with two files, editor should poll for the 2nd file
   x $HOME/projects/logon/etc/gdi/jpaconsole/cartridge/templates/createQuery4all.vm \
     /tmp/jpaconsole/createQuery4all.vm&
3. Replace the JPA query in the first file and save. JPA console will execute that
   query. Find the hibernate-generated SQL in the jpaconsole log. Find syntax error
   information also in the jpaconsole.


How to run static-tests (JS and CSS):
=========================================
JavaScript (JSHint):
	adnant test-static-js
		performs a full static test on all the JS files in the project
	adnant test-static-js-run -Dfile=/absolute/path/to/file/which/you/want/to/check.js -DoutputLogFile=/path/to/the/output/file/test-static-js.log
		performs the static check on a specific file; does not overwrite the existing log, so delete the old one!
CSS (CSSLint):
	adnant test-static-css
		performs a full static test on all the CSS files in the project

